\chapter{Ledger}\label{chap:ledger}

The Ledger concept owns the one law that makes this a ledger: a transaction is
a set of postings that sum to zero, and nothing else may be committed. It is
the only place in the system that knows this rule, which is what makes the
rule auditable --- to check that money is conserved, a reader looks in exactly
one function.

\section{The public seam}

A \texttt{Entry} is one posting: a signed amount, in cents, into one account.
A transaction is an array of them. \texttt{ledger\_post} is the only door
through which balances change as a group.

@d Ledger: header @{#ifndef LEDGER_LEDGER_H
#define LEDGER_LEDGER_H

typedef struct { int account; long amount; } Entry; /* one posting, cents */

/* Post a balanced transaction. Returns 0 on success; -1 if the entries do
   not sum to zero (the invariant) or name an account that does not exist.
   On failure NO account is modified --- the post is atomic. */
int ledger_post(const Entry *entries, int n);

#endif
@}

\section{Enforcing the invariant}

The implementation makes the atomicity promise real by validating everything
before it changes anything: first the postings must sum to zero, then every
referenced account must exist. Only once both checks pass does it apply the
deltas through \texttt{acc\_apply}. Because the store was validated up front,
the apply loop cannot fail partway and leave a half-posted transaction. This
is the single allowed dependency of Ledger on Accounts.

@d Ledger: implementation @{#include "ledger.h"
#include "accounts.h"

int ledger_post(const Entry *entries, int n) {
    long sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += entries[i].amount;
    if (sum != 0) return -1;                  /* the invariant: money is conserved */

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        if (!acc_exists(entries[i].account)) return -1;   /* validate before mutate */

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        acc_apply(entries[i].account, entries[i].amount); /* now safe: all valid */
    return 0;
}
@}

\section{Seed: the demo posting}

The walking skeleton's one transaction moves one hundred dollars: a debit to
\texttt{cash} and an equal credit to \texttt{revenue}. The two postings sum to
zero, so the ledger accepts it. Building the transaction is a Ledger concern,
so the snippet lives here; Api composes it after the demo accounts exist, and
it reads the \texttt{cash} and \texttt{revenue} ids from that scope.

@d Ledger: demo posting @{Entry txn[] = { { cash, +10000 }, { revenue, -10000 } };
    int rc = ledger_post(txn, 2);@}
